## Third-party source material

Some lessons derive from external educational material (Stanford lectures,
open-source documentation, published cheatsheets, the Pro Git book, etc.).
These sources retain their own licenses and are credited in each lesson's
`references.mdx` per the attribution policy in `Doc/attribution-policy.md`.

Examples currently in use or planned:

- **Pro Git** (Scott Chacon and Ben Straub), 2nd edition, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0
  Unported. The Clawdemy lesson-content license CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 is a
  compatible adaptation license for CC BY-NC-SA 3.0 source material under the
  3.0 license's "later versions" provision. See
  [git-scm.com/book](https://git-scm.com/book).
- **Stanford CME 295 cheatsheet** by Afshine & Shervine Amidi, MIT License.
  See [afshinea/stanford-cme-295-transformers-large-language-models](https://github.com/afshinea/stanford-cme-295-transformers-large-language-models).
- **Atlassian Git Tutorials**, CC BY 2.5 (permissive, no NC clause; can be
  incorporated into the more restrictive CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 derivative with
  preserved attribution).

Per the attribution policy, derivative lessons include the source license
notice in their `references.mdx` file.

---

## License history

Prior to 2026-06-11, lesson text content was licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0
International (commercial reuse permitted). The change to CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
reflects a clarification of Clawdemy's actual non-commercial intent. Content
distributed under the prior grant remains valid for recipients of that
distribution; the new license applies prospectively. See
`Doc/decisions.md` row 30 and `https://clawdemy.org/legal/licensing/` for the
detailed rationale and the commercial-licensing inquiry pathway.
